TD.</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TAKEUCHI, HISAO</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>MURABE KAORU</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>OTSUKI MAKOTO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>TAKEUCHI HISAO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MURABE, KAORU</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>OTSUKI, MAKOTO</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TAKEUCHI, HISAO</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A dynamic pressure bearing small in size and weight, simple in structure, and low in cost but capable of providing a sufficient rigidity, comprising a radial bearing part having grooves (26) to which fluid such as air can be led, and a thrust bearing part of which one end communicates with the radial bearing part and of which other end communicates with the outside, wherein the fluid led to the radial bearing part is led further into the thrust bearing part and, using a pressure of the fluid, a stable thrust force can be obtained without separately providing a thrust dynamic pressure generating mechanism, the surface area of the grooves (26) should be 40% or less of the entire surface area of the radial bearing part and the number of grooves (26) should desirably be six or less and, by making the grooves (26) in asymmetrical shape deeper at upstream part of air flow and shallower at downstream part thereof, the rigidity of the radial bearing part and the thrust bearing part can be increased.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un palier à pression dynamique dont les dimensions et le poids sont petits, la structure est simple et le coût est bas, mais qui peut présenter une rigidité suffisante. Il est constitué d'un palier radial pourvu de rainures (26), auxquelles on peut amener un fluide tel que l'air, et un palier de poussée dont l'une des extrémités communique avec l'extérieur. Le fluide amené au palier radial est guidé dans le palier de poussée et, à l'aide d'une pression du fluide, on obtient une poussée stable sans appliquer séparément un mécanisme générant une pression dynamique de poussée. La surface des rainures (26) devrait correspondre au maximum à 40 % de la surface totale du palier radial, leur nombre n'excédant pas, de préférence, six. Enfin, la présentation des rainures (26) en une forme asymétrique plus profonde en amont et moins profonde en aval du débit d'air, permet d'augmenter la rigidité du palier radial et du palier de poussée.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>